  <Instrument_Host>
    <name>Stardust Spacecraft</name>
    <type>Spacecraft</type>
    <naif_host_id>SDU</naif_host_id>
    <description>The Stardust spacecraft was a three-axis stabillized spacecraft measuring about 1.71m x 0.66m x 0.66m, with solar arrays mounted paralle to the long axis of the spacecraft body. In addition to its navigation camer, X-band telecommunications system, and two science instruments, Stardust carried an Aerogel plate, exposed toward comet 81P/Wild 2 to collect dust particles from that object. The Aerogel was returned to Earth by the spacecraft and the sample return capsule dropped to the surface. Following that, the Stardust spacecraft was redeployed in July 2007 for the NExT mission, to revisit comet 9P/Tempel 1, target of the Deep Impact mission, and observe the aftermath of that previous encounter. After that enounter (February 2011), the remaining fuel in the spacecraft was burned off as an engineering exercise and the transmitter turned off. The spacecraft continues in its heliocentric orbit.</description>
  </Instrument_Host>
